@namespace ExSheet
@implements IDisposable
@* Written in each component, not in an _Imports.razor, whose class Razor makes public (ADR-0201).
   Every product using is written with global::. A component in ExSheet.Components would look a
   plain "ExSheet.Engine" up against the ExSheet component class first, and a plain "ExGrid.X"
   against sibling types (the CS0426 trap recorded in AGENTS.md). The Web namespace makes @on...
   handlers compile as event handlers rather than as plain HTML attributes. *@
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web
@using global::ExGrid
@using global::ExGrid.Cells
@using global::ExGrid.Chrome
@using global::ExGrid.Components
@using global::ExGrid.Keys
@using global::ExGrid.Selection
@using global::ExSheet.Engine
@* Not the contract (ADR-0201): ExSheet renders this band when ShowToolbar is set, and no Consumer
   places it. It is public only because Razor makes every component public, is hidden from a
   Consumer's editor, and may change in any version. *@
@attribute [System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsable(System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsableState.Never)]

@*
    The Sheet Toolbar's band (ADR-0100), above the Formula Bar, inside the Sheet's box and outside the
    grid's root, so the grid's key listener never hears a key pressed here (ADR-0010, ADR-0018).

    It is ARIA's toolbar: one tab stop, which names the item the keyboard is on through
    aria-activedescendant. ← and → move among the items that can be pressed, Home and End go to the
    ends, Enter and Space press one, ↓ or Alt+↓ open a menu's or a split control's choices, and
    Escape gives the keyboard back to the Sheet. A press from the keys gives the keyboard back to
    the Sheet once its command has run. Its height is the rows' count times one row's, written
    inline (ADR-0028).

    Giving the keyboard back from here is the one subtle part. The core's focus function is granted
    only while DOM focus is inside the grid or on nothing (ADR-0021's note of 2026-09-30), and the
    toolbar is neither: so the toolbar names itself as the frame the keyboard comes back from, and the
    core takes it from there too (ADR-0050 item 16's note of 2026-10-12). On a circuit that lands a
    round trip after the key that asked for it, and a key typed meanwhile — on the band, or on an
    element a Chrome put the keyboard on inside it on the way out of its own list — would be the
    toolbar's: Enter pressed the last item again. From the moment the keyboard is on its way back the
    toolbar holds those keys, in order, and hands them to the grid once the core has the keyboard,
    which then answers them as it answers its own (ADR-0010). Nothing a key does depends on when it
    was typed. No script is added (ADR-0021).

    The KeyTips take the keyboard here the other way: the grid's key gate is told the keyboard is
    going to a frame of the Sheet's own, so the letters typed before the band holds it are held there
    and handed to the band in order (ADR-0050 item 16; ADR-0100's note of 2026-10-12).

    Escape goes back one level, as Excel's does (the Windows readings of 2026-10-12, R2 (b)): from a
    list a KeyTip opened to its row's letters, with the keyboard on the band, from a row's letters to
    the rows', and from the rows' to the Sheet; typed letters that narrow a level are a level of their
    own, as Excel's Format menu is behind O (observed, R2 (a): three Escapes after Alt, H, O left Excel
    Ready). A list closing is the keyboard in flight: the keys typed
    meanwhile, in the list itself or on the band, are held and handed on in order to where the keyboard
    lands (decided with the user, 2026-10-12). So is a list opening from the toolbar's keys: the keys
    typed until the list holds the keyboard are the list's, handed to it in order (decided with the
    user, 2026-10-13). The built-in list is the grid's popover: the band holds the keys for it, and the
    grid takes them with the keyboard from the band and hands them to its popover once that holds the
    keyboard (ADR-0050 item 16's note of 2026-10-12). A Chrome's own list, drawn outside the Sheet, joins
    the Sheet's Input Order while it is open (ToolbarItemContext.JoinSurface; ADR-0240 section 6, ticket
    06): the grid holds the keys behind the one that opened it until the list takes the keyboard through
    the grid (TakeKeyboard), and the keys behind the one that closes it, in its turn
    (ToolbarItemContext.ChoicesClosed), until the toolbar has taken the keyboard on from the list.

    The band answers only its own keys. A Chrome's control inside it that a Tab reaches — MudMenu's
    button — keeps the keys typed on it: none of them is held, and none presses another item.

    In the Sheet's Input Order (ADR-0240 section 6) the band claims its keys (data-ex-claims), and the
    grid holds the keys typed after one until it has been answered. While the band holds keys itself, for
    a list on its way, it says so (data-ex-holds): the grid then hands it the keys it held straight on,
    in order, for the band to pass to the list with its own.
*@
<div class="ex-sheet-toolbar" role="toolbar" aria-label="@SheetWords.ToolbarName" aria-orientation="horizontal"
     tabindex="0" style="@Style" aria-activedescendant="@ActiveId" @ref="_band" data-ex-claims="keydown" data-ex-holds="@(_typedOnTheWay is not null ? "keys" : null)"
     @onkeydown="OnBandKeyAsync" @onkeydown:preventDefault="@(_keysAreKeyTips || _typedOnTheWay is not null || _handingBack || _defaultsHeld)"
     @onkeyup="OnKeyUp" @onfocus="OnFocus" @onfocusout="OnFocusOut">
    @if (Context.KeyTips == KeyTipStage.Rows && Context.Rows.Count == 0)
    {
        <span class="ex-sheet-keytip ex-sheet-row-keytip" aria-hidden="true">@SheetToolbarContext.ImplicitRowKeyTip</span>
    }
    <CascadingValue Value="Context" IsFixed="true">
        <CascadingValue Value="_order" IsFixed="true">
            @(Content ?? DefaultRow)
        </CascadingValue>
    </CascadingValue>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>What the toolbar cascades.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public SheetToolbarContext Context { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>The Consumer's declared content; null for the default row.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Content { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The toolbar's height in pixels, which the Sheet subtracts from the grid's.</summary>
    [Parameter] public double HeightPx { get; set; }

    private static readonly RenderFragment DefaultRow = builder =>
    {
        builder.OpenComponent<DefaultToolbarRow>(0);
        builder.CloseComponent();
    };

    private string Style => FormattableString.Invariant($"height: {HeightPx}px; --ex-sheet-toolbar-row-height: {Context.RowHeightPx}px");

    private string? ActiveId => Context.Active?.Describe().Id;

    // The keys typed on the band, or on an element inside it, while the keyboard is in flight: held, in order, until it lands — on the Sheet, once the grid has it and asks
    // for them (HandKeysOn), or on this band, once it holds DOM focus (_heldForBand); null otherwise.
    private List<KeyboardEventArgs>? _typedOnTheWay;
    private bool _heldForBand;
    // The toolbar has asked the grid to take the keyboard back, and the grid has not yet asked for the keys.
    private bool _handingBack;
    // An item's list asked to open from the keys, whose keyboard has not yet landed in it: the keys held
    // meanwhile are its own (decided with the user, 2026-10-13).
    private ToolbarItemBase? _openingList;
    // DOM focus has left the band since the list was asked for: the list took the keyboard, or something
    // else did. Back on the band with the list still not saying it holds the keyboard, the hold ends.
    private bool _leftWhileOpening;
    // A Chrome's own list asked to open from the keys, which has not yet taken the keyboard: the keys typed
    // meanwhile are held by the grid for it, as for a frame of the Sheet's own (ADR-0240 section 6, ticket 06).
    private ToolbarItemBase? _ownListOpening;
    // The band is to take DOM focus, with the KeyTips standing: a focusout that comes first is the keyboard
    // on its way here, not leaving the toolbar.
    private bool _bandTakePending;
    private readonly Func<IReadOnlyList<KeyboardEventArgs>> _handKeysOn;

    /// <summary>The handler the grid asks for the held keys through, held in a field (AGENTS.md, the Blazor traps).</summary>
    public SheetToolbar() => _handKeysOn = HandKeysOn;

    private ElementReference _band;

    private bool KeyTipsShown => Context.KeyTips != KeyTipStage.Off;

    // From the KeyTips' start until the keyboard leaves the toolbar, every key typed on it is theirs,
    // its default too: an Alt released there would open the browser's menu. It holds past their
    // end, because the key that ends them gives the keyboard back to the Sheet while it is still
    // being dispatched, on WebAssembly before its default runs, and a default let through would type
    // that letter into the Sheet.
    private bool _keysAreKeyTips;

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Context.LeaveToolbar = GiveKeyboardBackAsync;
        Context.StartKeyTips = StartKeyTipsAsync;
        Context.ListOpeningHook = ListOpening;
        Context.ListTakesKeyboardHook = ListTakesKeyboardAsync;
        Context.ListOpenedInGridHook = ListOpenedInGridAsync;
        Context.ListClosedHook = ListClosedAsync;
        Context.ListEscapedHook = ListEscapedAsync;
        Context.Changed += OnToolbarChanged;
    }

    private bool _drawnKeyTips;

    private void OnToolbarChanged()
    {
        if (KeyTipsShown != _drawnKeyTips) _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Shows the rows' KeyTips and takes the keyboard to the toolbar (ADR-0100), as Excel's Alt
    /// takes it to the ribbon: the letters typed next are the KeyTips', never the cell's.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task StartKeyTipsAsync()
    {
        if (Context.Rows.Count > 0 && Context.Rows.All(row => row.KeyTip is null)) return;
        Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Rows);
        Context.KeyTipList = null;
        await BandTakesKeyboardFromGridAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The band takes the keyboard from the grid with the KeyTips standing: as they start, and as they
    /// come back from a list in the grid's popover. The grid's key gate is told before anything else that
    /// the keyboard is going to a frame of the Sheet's own, so the keys typed until the band holds it are
    /// held there and handed to it (ADR-0050 item 16; ADR-0100's note of 2026-10-12), and the band takes
    /// it through the grid, which gives it in turn, with the keys held for it: a key typed straight on the
    /// band never overtakes one still held. Both are told before this method first waits, which the gate's
    /// hold relies on: it waits only for its question to the core to be answered.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task BandTakesKeyboardFromGridAsync()
    {
        _keysAreKeyTips = true;
        _bandTakePending = true;
        var handedOff = Context.HandKeyboardToToolbar?.Invoke() ?? Task.CompletedTask;
        StateHasChanged();
        try
        {
            await (Context.GiveToolbarTheKeyboard?.Invoke(_band) ?? _band.FocusAsync(preventScroll: true).AsTask());
            // The band holds the keyboard already, or will once the grid's hold reaches it (OnFocus).
            if (_hasFocus) _bandTakePending = false;
            await handedOff;
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is Microsoft.JSInterop.JSException or Microsoft.JSInterop.JSDisconnectedException or ObjectDisposedException or OperationCanceledException)
        {
            _bandTakePending = false;
            Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Off);
        }
    }

    // A key while the KeyTips are shown (ADR-0100): a row's letter shows its items' letters, an
    // item's letters press it, Escape goes back one level and from the top ends them, and any other
    // key ends them and does nothing. The keyboard goes back to the Sheet when they end.
    private async Task OnKeyTipKeyAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        // Escape goes back one level (ADR-0100; the Windows readings of 2026-10-12, R2 (b)). Letters typed
        // that narrow a level are a level of their own, as Excel's Format menu is behind O (observed, R2 (a):
        // three Escapes after Alt, H, O left Excel Ready): Escape shows the level's every letter again. From
        // the rows' letters it ends them.
        if (e.Key == "Escape" && Context.KeyTipTyped.Length > 0)
        {
            Context.ShowKeyTips(Context.KeyTips, Context.KeyTipRow);
            return;
        }
        if (e.Key == "Escape" && Context.KeyTips == KeyTipStage.Items)
        {
            Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Rows);
            return;
        }
        if (e.Key.Length != 1 || e.CtrlKey || e.MetaKey || e.AltKey)
        {
            await EndKeyTipsAsync();
            return;
        }
        var typed = Context.KeyTipTyped + e.Key.ToUpperInvariant();
        if (Context.KeyTips == KeyTipStage.Rows)
        {
            var rows = Context.Rows.Count == 0
                ? [(Row: (ToolbarRow?)null, Letters: SheetToolbarContext.ImplicitRowKeyTip)]
                : Context.Rows.Where(row => row.KeyTip is not null).Select(row => (Row: (ToolbarRow?)row, Letters: row.KeyTip!)).ToList();
            if (rows.FirstOrDefault(r => string.Equals(r.Letters, typed, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)) is { Letters: not null } exact)
            {
                Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Items, exact.Row);
                return;
            }
            if (rows.Any(r => r.Letters.StartsWith(typed, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)))
            {
                Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Rows, null, typed);
                return;
            }
            await EndKeyTipsAsync();
            return;
        }
        var items = Context.Items.Where(item => ReferenceEquals(item.Row, Context.KeyTipRow) && item.KeyTipLetters is not null).ToList();
        if (items.FirstOrDefault(item => string.Equals(item.KeyTipLetters, typed, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)) is { } chosen)
        {
            Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Off);
            if (!chosen.CanPress)
            {
                await Context.ReturnKeyboardAsync();
                return;
            }
            // Excel's Alt, H, H opens the Fill's palette rather than setting its colour: a list opens.
            await chosen.PressFromKeyboardAsync(openChoices: true, fromKeyTip: true);
            return;
        }
        if (items.Any(item => item.KeyTipLetters!.StartsWith(typed, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)))
        {
            Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Items, Context.KeyTipRow, typed);
            return;
        }
        await EndKeyTipsAsync();
    }

    private async Task EndKeyTipsAsync()
    {
        _bandTakePending = false;
        Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Off);
        await Context.ReturnKeyboardAsync();
    }

    // ---- A list opening (decided with the user, 2026-10-13) ----

    /// <summary>
    /// An item's list is asked to open from the toolbar's keys: on a circuit it takes the keyboard a round
    /// trip later, and the keys typed meanwhile are its own. They are held from now. A Chrome's own list,
    /// drawn outside the Sheet, is a frame the keyboard is handed to: the grid holds the keys, behind the key
    /// that asked, until the list's control takes the keyboard through the grid, and hands them to it in
    /// order (ADR-0240 section 6, ticket 06). Told before the key's turn first waits, as the hold requires.
    /// The built-in frame's list is the grid's popover, which the band holds the keys for until the grid takes
    /// the keyboard from it (<see cref="ListOpenedInGridAsync"/>).
    /// </summary>
    private void ListOpening(ToolbarItemBase item, bool ownList)
    {
        if (ownList)
        {
            _ownListOpening = item;
            _ = Context.HandKeyboardToToolbar?.Invoke();
            return;
        }
        _openingList = item;
        _leftWhileOpening = false;
        if (_typedOnTheWay is null)
        {
            _typedOnTheWay = [];
            _heldForBand = false;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Chrome's own list hands the keyboard to one of its controls (<see cref="ToolbarItemContext.TakeKeyboard"/>):
    /// through the grid, which grants it while the keyboard is the Sheet's, and which, while it holds keys for
    /// the list, gives it in turn, with those keys.
    /// </summary>
    private Task ListTakesKeyboardAsync(ToolbarItemBase item, ElementReference control)
    {
        if (ReferenceEquals(_ownListOpening, item)) _ownListOpening = null;
        return Context.GiveToolbarTheKeyboard?.Invoke(control) ?? control.FocusAsync().AsTask();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The built-in frame's list was asked to open from the toolbar's keys: the grid takes the keyboard from
    /// the band, with the keys held for the list, and hands them to its popover once that holds the keyboard
    /// (ADR-0050 item 16's note of 2026-10-12: a keyboard already in the grid's popover stays there). A list
    /// that did not open leaves them to the band.
    /// </summary>
    private Task ListOpenedInGridAsync(ToolbarItemBase item, bool opened)
    {
        if (!ReferenceEquals(_openingList, item)) return Task.CompletedTask;
        if (!opened)
        {
            if (TakeOpeningHold(item, out var keys)) _ = AnswerOnBandAsync(keys);
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }
        _openingList = null;
        if (Context.TakeKeyboardBack is not { } takeBack) return Task.CompletedTask;
        _heldForBand = false;
        _handingBack = true;
        _typedOnTheWay ??= [];
        return takeBack(_band, _handKeysOn);
    }

    // The hold for a list opening, ended: its keys, unless a hand-back or a close has taken the hold over.
    private bool TakeOpeningHold(ToolbarItemBase item, out IReadOnlyList<KeyboardEventArgs> keys)
    {
        keys = [];
        if (!ReferenceEquals(_openingList, item)) return false;
        _openingList = null;
        if (_handingBack || _heldForBand) return false;
        keys = _typedOnTheWay ?? [];
        _typedOnTheWay = null;
        StateHasChanged();
        return true;
    }

    // Keys held for a list that did not take the keyboard: the band's own, in order.
    private Task AnswerOnBandAsync(IReadOnlyList<KeyboardEventArgs> keys) => AnswerInOrderAsync(keys);

    /// <summary>
    /// Keys held for the band, answered as typed there, in order. A key that sends the keyboard on — back
    /// to the Sheet, or into a list — holds the keys behind it from its first step, before it waits, as
    /// the band holds a key typed on it then: the rest join that hold at once, ahead of any key typed on
    /// the band while it waits, rather than after its wait, by when the grid may have asked for the keys
    /// held and been answered without them (on WebAssembly it asks within that first step, and
    /// <see cref="HandKeysOn"/> takes the rest with it). Answered one after another, Escape three times in
    /// a list a KeyTip opened, then Alt, H, H, typed at once, lost every key after the third Escape on the
    /// Server host (decided with the user, 2026-10-13: such keys have the outcome they have typed slowly).
    /// </summary>
    private async Task AnswerInOrderAsync(IReadOnlyList<KeyboardEventArgs> keys)
    {
        var rest = new Queue<KeyboardEventArgs>(keys);
        _answeringInOrder = rest;
        try
        {
            while (rest.TryDequeue(out var key))
            {
                var answered = OnKeyAsync(key);
                if (_typedOnTheWay is { } hold)
                {
                    hold.AddRange(rest);
                    rest.Clear();
                }
                await answered;
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            if (ReferenceEquals(_answeringInOrder, rest)) _answeringInOrder = null;
        }
    }

    // The keys of the band's own held keys not yet answered (AnswerInOrderAsync).
    private Queue<KeyboardEventArgs>? _answeringInOrder;

    // ---- A list closing (ADR-0100's note of 2026-10-12, decided with the user) ----

    /// <summary>
    /// A Chrome's list closes (<see cref="ToolbarItemContext.ChoicesClosed"/>), in the turn of the input that
    /// closed it, with the keyboard still in the list, or wherever a close by the design system's own means
    /// left it. The choice is made first, then the keyboard goes on: Escape in a list a KeyTip opened goes back
    /// to that row's KeyTips, as does any list closing while the KeyTips stand; anything else gives the
    /// keyboard back to the Sheet. The keys typed meanwhile are held by the grid behind that input and handed
    /// on where the keyboard landed (ADR-0240 section 6, ticket 06). A list that never took the keyboard it
    /// was asked to open with — it did not open — gives the keys held for it to the band, which holds the
    /// keyboard still.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ListClosedAsync(ToolbarItemBase item, ToolbarChoice? choice, bool escaped)
    {
        if (ReferenceEquals(_ownListOpening, item))
        {
            _ownListOpening = null;
            await GiveBandTheKeysHeldAsync();
            if (choice is null) return;
        }
        if (choice is not null)
        {
            // Format Cells opened by the choice waits for the keyboard to come back through the grid
            // (ExSheet's TakeKeyboardBackFromToolbarAsync).
            Context.ClosingList = true;
            try
            {
                await choice.Choose();
            }
            finally
            {
                Context.ClosingList = false;
            }
        }
        var fromKeyTip = ReferenceEquals(Context.KeyTipList, item);
        if (fromKeyTip) Context.KeyTipList = null;
        if (KeyTipsShown || (escaped && fromKeyTip))
        {
            if (!KeyTipsShown) Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Items, item.Row);
            await BandTakesKeyboardBackAsync();
            return;
        }
        await Context.ReturnKeyboardAsync();
    }

    // The keys the grid held for a Chrome's list that never took the keyboard: the band's, given the keyboard
    // it holds through the grid, which ends the hold for the list and hands them to it in order.
    private async Task GiveBandTheKeysHeldAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            await (Context.GiveToolbarTheKeyboard?.Invoke(_band) ?? Task.CompletedTask);
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is Microsoft.JSInterop.JSException or Microsoft.JSInterop.JSDisconnectedException or ObjectDisposedException or OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // The page went, or the circuit did.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Escape closed an item's list in the grid's popover (the built-in Chrome): one a KeyTip opened goes
    /// back to that row's KeyTips, and the band takes the keyboard from the grid, with the keys typed
    /// after the Escape; otherwise the grid keeps the keyboard it takes back.
    /// </summary>
    private Task ListEscapedAsync(ToolbarItemBase item)
    {
        if (!ReferenceEquals(Context.KeyTipList, item)) return Task.CompletedTask;
        Context.KeyTipList = null;
        Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Items, item.Row);
        return BandTakesKeyboardFromGridAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Escape on the band before the list a KeyTip opened has taken the keyboard: the list closes, as Escape
    /// in it would close it, and the KeyTips go back to its row's letters, with the keyboard on the band —
    /// taken back through the grid, in turn, should the list have taken it meanwhile.
    /// </summary>
    private Task BackFromKeyTipListAsync(ToolbarItemBase item)
    {
        Context.KeyTipList = null;
        if (ReferenceEquals(_ownListOpening, item)) _ownListOpening = null;
        item.RequestCloseOfChoices();
        Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Items, item.Row);
        return BandTakesKeyboardFromGridAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The band takes DOM focus back from an element inside it, with the KeyTips standing: the keys held,
    /// and those typed until it holds the keyboard, are its own, in order, once it does (OnFocus).
    /// </summary>
    private async Task BandTakesKeyboardBackAsync()
    {
        _openingList = null;
        _typedOnTheWay ??= [];
        _heldForBand = true;
        _handingBack = false;
        _keysAreKeyTips = true;
        _bandTakePending = true;
        StateHasChanged();
        try
        {
            // Through the grid, from a Chrome's list joined to the Sheet's Input Order: granted while the
            // keyboard is the Sheet's, so a press the user made elsewhere meanwhile keeps it there.
            await (Context.GiveToolbarTheKeyboard?.Invoke(_band) ?? _band.FocusAsync(preventScroll: true).AsTask());
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is Microsoft.JSInterop.JSException or Microsoft.JSInterop.JSDisconnectedException or ObjectDisposedException or OperationCanceledException)
        {
            _typedOnTheWay = null;
            _heldForBand = false;
            _bandTakePending = false;
            Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Off);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Gives the keyboard back to the Sheet from the toolbar: the core takes it from the band, or from
    /// an element inside it, and the keys typed there meanwhile are held and handed to the grid.
    /// </summary>
    private Task GiveKeyboardBackAsync()
    {
        _bandTakePending = false;
        // A list on its way: its keys are the Sheet's now, with the keyboard (CloseRequest). A Chrome's own
        // list asked to close so tells nothing more (ToolbarItemContext.CloseRequest).
        _openingList = null;
        _ownListOpening = null;
        if (Context.TakeKeyboardBack is not { } takeBack) return Context.ReturnKeyboardToSheetAsync();
        // The keys held for this band are the Sheet's now, ahead of any
        // typed after. The keys typed on the band from here are not held by the band: each key typed on it
        // is in the Sheet's Input Order, and the grid holds the keys after the one that gave the keyboard
        // back until that one has been answered, then hands them on where the keyboard landed (ADR-0240
        // section 6, which replaced the band's own hold here).
        _heldForBand = false;
        _handingBack = true;
        // Drawn before the key that gave the keyboard back has finished its dispatch: the browser reads
        // whether to prevent the key's default once the handler has run, and by then the keyboard can be
        // in the grid's Keyboard Field, where a character's default would type it again.
        StateHasChanged();
        return takeBack(_band, _handKeysOn);
    }

    // The grid has the keyboard, or was declined it: the keys held on the way are its, and the band's
    // keys are its own again.
    private IReadOnlyList<KeyboardEventArgs> HandKeysOn()
    {
        IReadOnlyList<KeyboardEventArgs> keys = _typedOnTheWay ?? [];
        // Asked for within the first step of a held key that sent the keyboard on, before the keys held
        // behind it could join its hold: they go with it, in order (AnswerInOrderAsync).
        if (_answeringInOrder is { Count: > 0 } rest)
        {
            keys = [.. keys, .. rest];
            rest.Clear();
        }
        _typedOnTheWay = null;
        _handingBack = false;
        _openingList = null;
        _ownListOpening = null;
        _defaultsHeld = true;
        _ = LetDefaultsGoAsync();
        return keys;
    }

    // The key whose press gave the keyboard back may still be being dispatched: on WebAssembly the grid
    // takes the keyboard, and asks for the keys, inside that key's own handler, and Blazor reads the
    // band's preventDefault after the handler has run, so a default let through then would type that
    // key into the Sheet, which now has the keyboard. The band's defaults, and the KeyTips' hold on
    // them, go once the key has been dispatched.
    private bool _defaultsHeld;

    private async Task LetDefaultsGoAsync()
    {
        await Task.Yield();
        _defaultsHeld = false;
        if (_typedOnTheWay is null && !KeyTipsShown) _keysAreKeyTips = false;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    // Noted as the render is drawn, not after it: see ToolbarRow.
    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override bool ShouldRender()
    {
        _drawnKeyTips = KeyTipsShown;
        return true;
    }

    private bool _hasFocus;

    /// <inheritdoc />
    public void Dispose()
    {
        Context.Changed -= OnToolbarChanged;
        if (Context.LeaveToolbar == GiveKeyboardBackAsync) Context.LeaveToolbar = null;
        if (Context.StartKeyTips == StartKeyTipsAsync) Context.StartKeyTips = null;
        if (Context.ListOpeningHook == ListOpening) Context.ListOpeningHook = null;
        if (Context.ListTakesKeyboardHook == ListTakesKeyboardAsync) Context.ListTakesKeyboardHook = null;
        if (Context.ListOpenedInGridHook == ListOpenedInGridAsync) Context.ListOpenedInGridHook = null;
        if (Context.ListClosedHook == ListClosedAsync) Context.ListClosedHook = null;
        if (Context.ListEscapedHook == ListEscapedAsync) Context.ListEscapedHook = null;
        Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Off);
    }

    private async Task OnFocus()
    {
        _hasFocus = true;
        _bandTakePending = false;
        // A list on its way keeps its hold while the band holds the keyboard it was asked from. The keyboard
        // back on the band after leaving it, with the built-in list never having taken it (a list that never
        // drew), ends the hold: the keys are the band's, in order, rather
        // than held from a band that has the keyboard (a trap).
        if (_openingList is { } opening && _leftWhileOpening && !_heldForBand && !_handingBack)
        {
            if (TakeOpeningHold(opening, out var keys)) await AnswerOnBandAsync(keys);
        }
        else if (_typedOnTheWay is { } held && (_heldForBand || !_handingBack) && (_openingList is null || _heldForBand))
        {
            // The keyboard has landed here: the keys held for the band are its own, in order — one of them
            // giving the keyboard back holds the rest for the Sheet. A hold nothing is handing back holds
            // no more: its keys are dropped, rather than kept from a toolbar that has the keyboard (a trap)
            // or given to a place they were not typed for.
            var forBand = _heldForBand;
            _typedOnTheWay = null;
            _heldForBand = false;
            StateHasChanged();
            if (forBand) await AnswerInOrderAsync(held);
        }
        // Tab arrives on the item the keyboard was on last, else on the first that can be pressed.
        if (Context.Active is { CanPress: true } || KeyTipsShown) return;
        Context.Activate(Pressable().FirstOrDefault());
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    private void OnFocusOut(FocusEventArgs _)
    {
        _hasFocus = false;
        if (_openingList is not null) _leftWhileOpening = true;
        // The KeyTips end when the keyboard leaves: a list an item opened, or a press elsewhere. Not while
        // the band is taking the keyboard itself, from an element inside it or one the grid hands it from.
        if (_bandTakePending) return;
        Context.ShowKeyTips(KeyTipStage.Off);
        if (_keysAreKeyTips && _typedOnTheWay is null)
        {
            _keysAreKeyTips = false;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
        // The highlight goes with DOM focus; the place is kept for the next Tab.
        if (Context.Active is null) return;
        _lastActive = Context.Active;
        Context.Activate(null);
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    private ToolbarItemBase? _lastActive;

    // The scope the toolbar's rows and items take their places from, in markup order.
    private readonly ToolbarOrder _order = new();

    private IEnumerable<ToolbarItemBase> Pressable() => Context.Items.Where(item => item.CanPress);

    // The Alt key's release typed on the band while the keyboard is in flight: held in its place among the
    // keys, so that it reaches the Sheet after the press it releases (decided with the user, 2026-10-13).
    // The Sheet's own keyup handler, on the element around the band, hears it next and leaves it.
    private void OnKeyUp(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key != "Alt" || _typedOnTheWay is not { } held) return;
        held.Add(e);
        Context.KeyUpHeld = true;
    }

    // A key typed on the band, in its place in the Sheet's Input Order (ADR-0240 section 6): the band
    // claims its keys (data-ex-claims), and the grid's listener, which hears the whole Sheet, numbers each
    // as it goes on and holds the keys typed after it until it has been answered. Every key is put in the
    // order, the band's own or not: a number told and never taken would hold every input behind it.
    private Task OnBandKeyAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e) => Context.InOrderAsync(GridInputKind.KeyDown, () => OnKeyAsync(e));

    private async Task OnKeyAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        // A release held among the keys is the Sheet's, not the band's: answered on the band, it goes on
        // with the keys if the keyboard is in flight again — an Alt's release held behind the Escape that
        // ends the KeyTips — so it reaches the Sheet after its press, and is dropped otherwise (decided with
        // the user, 2026-10-13).
        if (e.Type == "keyup")
        {
            _typedOnTheWay?.Add(e);
            return;
        }
        Context.Sheet.ForgetAltPress();
        // While the keyboard is in flight — back to the Sheet, or on its way here — the keys are held, in
        // order, for where it lands. Only then: a hold stands only while a hand-back is under way.
        if (_typedOnTheWay is { } onTheWay)
        {
            onTheWay.Add(e);
            return;
        }
        // The band answers its own keys only. One typed on a control inside it that holds the keyboard
        // itself — a Chrome's own, a Tab reaches MudMenu's button — is that control's, and presses nothing.
        if (!_hasFocus) return;
        if (KeyTipsShown)
        {
            await OnKeyTipKeyAsync(e);
            return;
        }
        // Escape before the list a KeyTip opened has the keyboard: the list closes and the KeyTips go back
        // a level, as Escape in the list does (R2 (b)).
        if (e.Key == "Escape" && Context.KeyTipList is { } keyTipList)
        {
            await BackFromKeyTipListAsync(keyTipList);
            return;
        }
        var items = Pressable().ToList();
        if (items.Count == 0)
        {
            if (e.Key == "Escape") await Context.ReturnKeyboardAsync();
            return;
        }
        var active = Context.Active ?? _lastActive;
        var at = active is null ? -1 : items.IndexOf(active);
        switch (e.Key)
        {
            case "ArrowRight":
                Move(items[at < 0 || at == items.Count - 1 ? 0 : at + 1]);
                break;
            case "ArrowLeft":
                Move(items[at <= 0 ? items.Count - 1 : at - 1]);
                break;
            case "Home":
                Move(items[0]);
                break;
            case "End":
                Move(items[^1]);
                break;
            case "Enter" or " " when at >= 0:
                await items[at].PressFromKeyboardAsync(openChoices: false);
                break;
            case "ArrowDown" when at >= 0:
                await items[at].PressFromKeyboardAsync(openChoices: true);
                break;
            case "Escape":
                await Context.ReturnKeyboardAsync();
                break;
        }
    }

    private void Move(ToolbarItemBase item)
    {
        Context.Activate(item);
        StateHasChanged();
    }
}
